import { FilesystemReaderCopy } from "../model"

pub fn copy -> FilesystemReaderCopy = FilesystemReaderCopy {
  summary: "Create a dedicated temporary directory, pass it to work, and clean it up afterward. Return the result you need from that work.",
  taskTitle: "Create a temporary report and return its content",
  purpose: "Write ready to report.txt in a temporary directory, read it back, and return the content. Then run separate work that returns an ordinary failure and display that result too. The lasting result is the content, not a path for opening the temporary file later.",
  setup: "fs, paths, and effects are imported modules. effect fn report directory: paths.Path -> String defines work taking directory and producing String when executed. with FileSystem is its file service requirement; fails ReportError names ordinary failures. withTemporaryDirectory takes a name prefix and a function that receives the created Path. That function is called the callback.",
  after: [
    "report uses paths.child to construct the Path for report.txt. Left carries a problem and Right a Path. Passing the result through |> to effects.fromEither makes executable work. Within do, <- receives file, _ <- waits for the CreateNew write, and the last operation reads the content. ReportError’s PathFailure, WriteFailure, and ReadFailure retain the original problems, wrapped with mapError. deriving Show enables display through show.",
    "The first call in main creates a directory with a unique suffix after fs16-success-. The syntax \\directory -> ... is an unnamed function taking directory. report directory |> effects.mapError show converts ordinary failures to display Strings while leaving successful content, ready, unchanged.",
    "reject also takes directory, but creates no file. effects.fail \"report rejected\" returns an ordinary String failure. The second withTemporaryDirectory receives reject. In the checked executions, the created directories are cleaned up after both the successful callback and this failed one.",
    "effects.attempt turns overall success into Right or ordinary failure into Left. describe’s parameter type, Either<Either<fs.FileSystemError, String>, String>, describes that result. An outer Right text means overall success. An outer Left failure means failure: its inner Left error indicates acquisition or cleanup trouble, while its inner Right reason identifies callback failure.",
    "The inner Right is not overall success. describe’s two match expressions separate the origin of a failure. Success returns success: ready; reject returns callback failure: report rejected. show converts values to text and ${...} inserts them into strings. In main, _ <- println finishes the first print before continuing; the final println displays the second result."
  ],
  details: [
    "Use a single name as prefix. Invalid inputs such as empty text, ., .., separators, or NUL (the zero character) fail with CreateTemporary and PathNotSupported. A randomly generated suffix and exclusive creation avoid reusing an existing directory. The FileSystem service configures the temporary root. If acquisition fails, the callback is not called.",
    "If the callback succeeds but cleanup fails, the whole operation fails with a FileSystemError in Left. An ordinary callback failure goes in Right. In the declaration, E is the callback’s failure type and A its success type. R & { fileSystem: FileSystem } adds FileSystem to the services that the callback already requires.",
    "Returning a Path inside the temporary directory does not extend its lifetime. The file is removed after ordinary completion, so obtain and return the lasting value during the callback, as the content example does. Cleanup targets the directory and contents created by this operation, without following symbolic links to other locations. Cleanup succeeds if it was already removed and refuses to delete a different resource placed at the same path. Trying to move an active temporary root through standard file operations fails with PermissionDenied. Constructing the work does not create a directory; executing its returned Effect does.",
    "A separate targeted check explored simultaneous failure beyond the ordinary examples above. In the Node/Bun service implementation, replacing the temporary resource with a different resource at the same path caused cleanup to refuse deletion. When the callback also failed, the direct result retained its original failure inside Right, but later service shutdown raised a separate ProviderPackageDefect. The cleanup diagnostic attachment required by the specification was not observed in the inspected return value or warning output.",
    "Therefore, this page does not present diagnostic attachment during simultaneous failure, or reporting only the original failure through process exit, as an established implementation guarantee. Cleanup during cancellation, unexpected runtime problems, or forced termination is also not established by this example’s success and ordinary-failure checks."
  ],
  comparisonBefore: "The TS withWorkspace helper creates a directory with mkdtemp, awaits the callback, and runs rm in finally. An async function returns its eventual result as a Promise. For ordinary success or callback failure with successful cleanup, it produces the same content and failure message.",
  comparisonAfter: "Seseragi provides creation, callback execution, and cleanup as one operation, with values distinguishing the origin of ordinary failure. TS can express cleanup with try/finally too. A failure in finally replaces an earlier failure, so the comparison does not establish matching simultaneous-failure behavior. The unusual targeted observation below is also outside what this short comparison proves."
}
